# Roadbook pilot front — Caddyfile template (phase 8 BRIEF §3B).
#
# Shape: Tailscale Funnel terminates public TLS on the serving machine and
# hands each funnel port (443 / 8443 / 10000) to one plain-HTTP loopback
# listener below. Each listener enforces exactly one tester's basic-auth
# credential and proxies to that tester's instance web port. One block per
# active tester; three concurrent testers maximum (the funnel port ceiling,
# BRIEF §1.5). Listener ports 8100/8101/8102 pair with funnel ports
# 443/8443/10000 by convention.
#
# Instantiate OUTSIDE the repository or under docs/private/ (gitignored) and
# point the system Caddyfile at it with an `import`. Never commit an
# instantiated copy: credential hashes, tester names, and any real hostname
# stay private (the public-repo rule, BRIEF §2). Hash a credential with:
#
#   caddy hash-password
#
# Bring a listener live (one funnel port each):
#
#   tailscale funnel --bg --https=443   8100
#   tailscale funnel --bg --https=8443  8101
#   tailscale funnel --bg --https=10000 8102
#
# The credential, not the URL, is the secret: funnel hostnames appear in
# public Certificate Transparency logs (BRIEF §1.3). A listener without
# basic_auth must never front an instance holding anyone's real data.
#
# Field-tested trap (CP1, real phone): mobile keyboards auto-append a space
# after a typed or suggested username, and "user " fails bcrypt exactly like
# a wrong password — an endless re-prompt with no clue. The handover message
# must say "no space after the username"; when diagnosing a login loop,
# check for the trailing space before anything else.

# The address is port-only ON PURPOSE, and the bind is loopback ON PURPOSE.
# `http://127.0.0.1:8100` as the ADDRESS would make 127.0.0.1 a HOST
# MATCHER, so funnel traffic (Host: <machine>.ts.net) would fall through to
# Caddy's empty default response instead of this block — found live at CP1:
# auth "worked" locally and served nothing publicly. A port-only address
# alone would bind all interfaces — the stale-dev-server lesson (phase 7
# LOG). `bind 127.0.0.1` separates the two: any Host, loopback socket only;
# the funnel remains the sole public path.
http://:8100 {
	bind 127.0.0.1
	basic_auth {
		{TESTER_USER} {BCRYPT_HASH}
	}
	# Belt-and-braces behind auth: never indexed, never previewed.
	header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{INSTANCE_WEB_PORT}
}
